[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Optional Parameters in Lambda Expressions in C#"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"C# allows you to define optional parameters in methods. However, using optional parameters directly within lambda expressions requires a slightly different approach because lambda expressions are essentially anonymous methods or delegates. This tutorial will guide you through how to effectively use optional parameters with lambda expressions."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Challenge"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Lambda expressions are concise ways to create anonymous functions. While you can't directly define optional parameters within the lambda expression's parameter list, you can achieve similar behavior by using default values within the lambda's body or by creating wrapper functions."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Method 1: Default Values within the Lambda Body"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"One way to simulate optional parameters is to check for null or default values within the lambda expression's body. If a value isn't provided, you can use a default value."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Using default values within the lambda body
Func<string, string> greet = name => name == null ? "Hello, Guest!" : $"Hello, {name}!";

Console.WriteLine(greet(null)); // Output: Hello, Guest!
Console.WriteLine(greet("Alice")); // Output: Hello, Alice!"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `greet` lambda expression takes a `string` parameter `name`. If `name` is `null`, it defaults to "Hello, Guest!". Otherwise, it uses the provided name."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Method 2: Using Wrapper Functions"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Another approach is to create a wrapper function that calls the lambda expression with a default value if the parameter is not provided. This is useful when you want to maintain a cleaner lambda expression and handle the optional parameter logic separately."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Using a wrapper function
public static class LambdaExtensions
{
    public static string Greet(this Func<string, string> greeter, string name = "Guest")
    {
        return greeter(name);
    }
}

Func<string, string> greetLambda = name => $"Hello, {name}!";

Console.WriteLine(greetLambda.Greet()); // Output: Hello, Guest!
Console.WriteLine(greetLambda.Greet("Bob")); // Output: Hello, Bob!"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, we define an extension method `Greet` that takes an optional `name` parameter with a default value of "Guest". This method then calls the `greetLambda` lambda expression with the provided or default name. This approach keeps the lambda expression simple and moves the optional parameter handling to the extension method."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Method 3: Overloading Delegates (Less Common)"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"While less common, you could potentially overload delegates to achieve a similar effect, but this is generally not recommended for simple optional parameters as it can lead to more complex code."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Overloading delegates (Not Recommended for Simple Cases)
public delegate string Greeter();
public delegate string GreeterWithName(string name);

public static class GreeterFactory
{
    public static GreeterWithName CreateGreeter(string name)
    {
        return (n) => $"Hello, {n}!";
    }

    public static Greeter CreateGreeter()
    {
        return () => "Hello, Guest!";
    }
}

GreeterWithName greetWithName = GreeterFactory.CreateGreeter("Alice");
Greeter greet = GreeterFactory.CreateGreeter();

Console.WriteLine(greet()); // Output: Hello, Guest!
Console.WriteLine(greetWithName("Charlie")); // Output: Hello, Charlie!"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"This approach involves creating separate delegate types and factory methods to handle different parameter signatures. It's generally better to use the default value or wrapper function approaches for simpler optional parameter scenarios."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"While C# doesn't directly support optional parameters within lambda expressions, you can effectively simulate this behavior by using default values within the lambda's body or by creating wrapper functions. The wrapper function approach often provides a cleaner separation of concerns and is generally recommended for more complex scenarios. Avoid overloading delegates unless absolutely necessary, as it can increase code complexity."}]
